{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KGdiXFhbEiJs5FFyy4WUyi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Contributing\n2\t\n3\tYou only need this section to work on Djinn itself; to use it, see the top of this file.\n4\t\n5\t**Prerequisites**\n6\t- [Go](https://go.dev/dl/) 1.25 or later, with CGO enabled for the native window.\n7\t- Node.js 22 and npm, for the interface (`npm ci` once).\n8\t- The native window uses [Wails v3](https://v3.wails.io) (v3.0.0-beta.28):\n9\t  - **Linux**: GTK and WebKitGTK headers and a C compiler. On Ubuntu 22.04:\n10\t    `sudo apt install build-essential libgtk-3-dev libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev`, and build with the\n11\t    `gtk3` tag. Newer distributions use GTK 4 and `webkitgtk-6.0`.\n12\t  - **macOS**: the Xcode Command Line Tools (`xcode-select --install`): Apple's C compiler\n13\t    and the system headers, which the window needs to talk to Cocoa and WebKit. Not the Xcode\n14\t    app. Most Macs that ever ran `git` or Homebrew have them already: `xcode-select -p` tells.\n15\t  - **Windows**: WebView2, shipped with Windows 11.\n16\t- Nothing else to install: Task, buf and the protobuf generators are tools of the Go module.\n17\t\n18\t**Everyday commands** (`go tool task --list` shows them all)\n19\t- `go tool task gen`: generate the code from the protos in `api/`.\n20\t- `go tool task test`: the unit tests, in seconds, with nothing running.\n21\t- `go tool task e2e`: the end-to-end tests, in a headless browser against the real `djinn`.\n22\t- `go tool task build`: build the interface, then the `djinn` binary with its native window\n23\t  (CGO and the system webview; the task adds the `gtk3` tag on Ubuntu 22.04). Then `bin/djinn up`.\n24\t  The terminal at the bottom of the window runs your shell in your home directory;\n25\t  `bin/djinn up --terminal \"claude --resume <session>\" --terminal-dir <project>` runs that command\n26\t  there instead, through your shell. It lives as long as `djinn up`, and hangs up when it stops.\n27\t- `go tool task check-window`: open a native window for a few seconds and check that a stream\n28\t  reaches it value by value, with its latency, on the path this system uses (`wails://` on macOS\n29\t  and Linux, loopback HTTP on Windows); `-- -path http` or `-- -path wails` checks the other one.\n30\t  Prints PASS or FAIL.\n31\t- Tests and CI build with `-tags headless`: no native window, no CGO.\n32\t\n33\t**Dependencies, and thanks**\n34\t\n35\tWe keep as few dependencies as we can: each one must earn its place, and its license must allow\n36\tit (see [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md)). Djinn stands on the work of these projects; thank you to all\n37\ttheir contributors.\n38\t\n39\t- Go: [Wails](https://github.com/wailsapp/wails), [Connect](https://github.com/connectrpc/connect-go),\n40\t  [protobuf-go](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf-go),\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a118cf-7bb0-72dc-9be9-cd29b496dca9","uuid":"069702a4-3d7d-44b5-9cc1-eec53669ba86","timestamp":"2026-10-08T00:00:15.463Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/work/project/SETUP.md","content":"## Contributing\n\nYou only need this section to work on Djinn itself; to use it, see the top of this file.\n\n**Prerequisites**\n- [Go](https://go.dev/dl/) 1.25 or later, with CGO enabled for the native window.\n- Node.js 22 and npm, for the interface (`npm ci` once).\n- The native window uses [Wails v3](https://v3.wails.io) (v3.0.0-beta.28):\n  - **Linux**: GTK and WebKitGTK headers and a C compiler. On Ubuntu 22.04:\n    `sudo apt install build-essential libgtk-3-dev libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev`, and build with the\n    `gtk3` tag. Newer distributions use GTK 4 and `webkitgtk-6.0`.\n  - **macOS**: the Xcode Command Line Tools (`xcode-select --install`): Apple's C compiler\n    and the system headers, which the window needs to talk to Cocoa and WebKit. Not the Xcode\n    app. Most Macs that ever ran `git` or Homebrew have them already: `xcode-select -p` tells.\n  - **Windows**: WebView2, shipped with Windows 11.\n- Nothing else to install: Task, buf and the protobuf generators are tools of the Go module.\n\n**Everyday commands** (`go tool task --list` shows them all)\n- `go tool task gen`: generate the code from the protos in `api/`.\n- `go tool task test`: the unit tests, in seconds, with nothing running.\n- `go tool task e2e`: the end-to-end tests, in a headless browser against the real `djinn`.\n- `go tool task build`: build the interface, then the `djinn` binary with its native window\n  (CGO and the system webview; the task adds the `gtk3` tag on Ubuntu 22.04). Then `bin/djinn up`.\n  The terminal at the bottom of the window runs your shell in your home directory;\n  `bin/djinn up --terminal \"claude --resume <session>\" --terminal-dir <project>` runs that command\n  there instead, through your shell. It lives as long as `djinn up`, and hangs up when it stops.\n- `go tool task check-window`: open a native window for a few seconds and check that a stream\n  reaches it value by value, with its latency, on the path this system uses (`wails://` on macOS\n  and Linux, loopback HTTP on Windows); `-- -path http` or `-- -path wails` checks the other one.\n  Prints PASS or FAIL.\n- Tests and CI build with `-tags headless`: no native window, no CGO.\n\n**Dependencies, and thanks**\n\nWe keep as few dependencies as we can: each one must earn its place, and its license must allow\nit (see [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md)). Djinn stands on the work of these projects; thank you to all\ntheir contributors.\n\n- Go: [Wails](https://github.com/wailsapp/wails), [Connect](https://github.com/connectrpc/connect-go),\n  [protobuf-go](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf-go),\n","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KGdiXFhbEiJs5FFyy4WUyi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
